                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_cash:          `<strong>That is the constraint that kills first.</strong> A £1.4M ramp against a £0.45M buffer on 75-day terms means you fund 2.5 months of growth before the first big invoice lands. Capacity and the founder matter — but cash is what makes you insolvent in month three while still profitable on paper.`,
                    coach_r1_founder:       `<strong>The founder bottleneck is real and severe.</strong> Every recipe and spend over £2,000 routing through Priya at triple volume is physically impossible. But ask what fails <em>first</em>: even with perfect delegation, the £1.4M ramp on a £0.45M buffer runs out of cash before the bottleneck burns her out.`,
                    coach_r1_concentration: `<strong>Concentration is a genuine resilience threat.</strong> Northgate at 40% of revenue gains pricing and exit power. But that bites at renewal, months out — what threatens survival <em>first</em> is funding the £1.4M ramp on a £0.45M buffer.`,
                    coach_r1_capacity:      `<strong>Capacity is binding — one site at 82% can't absorb triple volume.</strong> But a new line or outsourcing can fix capacity; what you cannot fix without a plan is paying for the ramp before Northgate pays you. Cash is the first killer.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You ranked cash as the first killer and named insolvency as the survival risk — profitable on paper, out of cash in month three. That is exactly the lens that forces you to fund the gap before you sign.`,
                    coach_r1_norisk:        `<strong>That is how the company dies.</strong> '£6.2M solves everything' is funding-gap blindness — the revenue arrives on 75-day terms, long after the £1.4M of ramp spend. Profitable growth that runs out of cash still kills the business. Name the real risk.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandor:     `<strong>Low candour now becomes denial later.</strong> If the team won't admit the business isn't ready to scale as-is — that growth eats cash and the founder is the ceiling — you'll sign Northgate on optimism and discover the constraints the hard way.`,
                    coach_r2_phased:        `<strong>Phased acceptance is disciplined growth.</strong> Starting at 120 stores and ramping over 8 months keeps the cash gap survivable and gives delegation time to land — you trade some speed for materially better survival odds. The right growth rate is bounded by cash and capacity.`,
                    coach_r2_full:          `<strong>You've signed for the full £6.2M at once.</strong> Maximum revenue, but the £1.4M ramp now hits a £0.45M buffer in one go, the SLA penalties bite while you're stretched, and Northgate is instantly 40% of revenue. You've grown and become fragile in the same stroke — make sure the finance round can carry it.`,
                    coach_r2_online:        `<strong>Declining for the resilient online channel is defensible — but mind the other risk.</strong> You keep control and margin, yet a competitor takes Northgate and your online momentum needs investment to stay ahead. Pure caution is also a risk; make sure you've actually built a growth engine, not just stayed small.`,
                    coach_r2_blended:       `<strong>A blended path spreads the risk.</strong> Partial Northgate plus continued online growth captures real scale while keeping concentration and the cash gap more manageable than the full deal — a credible middle path if the finance and delegation back it.`,
                    coach_r2_newline:       `<strong>The new chilled line protects margin and quality.</strong> It consumes £0.6M of cash now, so the financing round must cover it — but it means the Northgate volume is made to standard rather than stretched or outsourced at quality risk.`,
                    coach_r2_stretch:       `<strong>Stretching a site already at 82% past triple volume breaks it.</strong> You'll miss SLAs at £8,000 a time and damage the brand on the Northgate line. Capacity for that volume has to be built or bought — it can't be wished out of an over-run site.`,
                    coach_r2_noprotect:     `<strong>You've taken the volume with no protection.</strong> Northgate is now 40% of revenue and at the first renewal they'll squeeze your price or threaten to walk — and they can take 40% of your business with them. Volume caps, minimum terms and diversification are how growth doesn't become fragility.`,
                    coach_r3_targeted:      `<strong>Targeted delegation breaks the bottleneck the right way.</strong> Moving spend authority, key accounts and hiring out of Priya's hands while she keeps recipe sign-off frees the capacity to scale without losing what only the founder should own.`,
                    coach_r3_hold:          `<strong>You scaled the revenue and not the decision-making.</strong> If Priya still signs off everything, decisions stack up in week two of the ramp, a service failure triggers an £8,000 penalty, and she's heading for an 90-hour-week burnout. You can't triple volume through one person.`,
                    coach_r3_all:           `<strong>Handing off everything at once is risky.</strong> Delegating recipes, accounts, spend and hiring in the same month overwhelms new people — sequence it and keep what only Priya should own, or quality and brand take the hit.`,
                    coach_r3_governed:      `<strong>That is delegation, not abdication.</strong> Targeted handover backed by documented decision rights, thresholds and standards means the new people have something to decide <em>against</em> — the founder's judgement is replaced by a system, not just removed.`,
                    coach_r3_nogov:         `<strong>Delegation with no governance is abdication.</strong> Move decisions out of Priya's hands with no thresholds and no documented standards and quality slips on the Northgate line with nobody able to tell where it went wrong. Install decision rights, not just absence.`,
                    coach_r3_abdication:    `<strong>Everything delegated at once with no standards — that's abdication.</strong> New people, new responsibilities, no thresholds, no documented standards: a quality slip on the Northgate line and Priya can't tell where it went wrong. Empowerment needs governance.`,
                    coach_r3_nohire:        `<strong>No senior hire leaves the bottleneck in place.</strong> The existing team is already at capacity; without the £65k operations director there is no one to actually take the delegated decisions, so they route back to Priya. Hiring ahead of revenue is how you build the capacity to scale.`,
                    coach_r3_nostandards:   `<strong>You've let the standards go.</strong> Recipe and brand standards are exactly what Priya must never fully delegate — drop them and the thing customers buy you for slips on the very line that now carries 40% of your revenue.`,
                    coach_r4_loan:          `<strong>A bank loan preserves your equity.</strong> £1.0M at ~9% with no dilution keeps Priya at 100% — but it adds a fixed repayment the ramp's early, cash-light months must service. Make sure the path you chose can carry the debt without a second cash crunch.`,
                    coach_r4_equity:        `<strong>Equity adds resilience as well as cash.</strong> £1.5M for 25% dilutes Priya but brings a growth-fund partner's expertise and a cushion that debt can't — the structure strengthens survival odds, not just funds the ramp. Just be sure you raised it at the right moment.`,
                    coach_r4_blend:         `<strong>A blend bridges the gap pragmatically.</strong> A smaller loan plus stretched supplier terms covers much of the £1.4M need without full dilution or full repayment risk — a sensible middle structure if it genuinely closes the gap before the first Northgate payment.`,
                    coach_r4_bootstrap:     `<strong>Bootstrapping a £1.4M ramp on a £0.45M buffer is wishful.</strong> Supplier terms and cash flow won't bridge 2.5 months of triple-volume spend before the first 75-day payment. This is funding-gap blindness with extra steps — you'll be insolvent while profitable.`,
                    coach_r4_before:        `<strong>Raising before the ramp bridges the 75-day gap.</strong> Securing the cash up front means the ramp doesn't stall and you're not insolvent in month three — you accept terms now in exchange for surviving the gap. For an owner-managed firm at this point, certainty of survival usually beats squeezing valuation.`,
                    coach_r4_proof:         `<strong>Raising after early traction is shrewd here — because your cash survives the wait.</strong> Proving the Northgate line first earns you a better valuation, and you've kept enough runway to bridge the gap meanwhile. That's timing the raise against valuation without betting the company.`,
                    coach_r4_proof_risky:   `<strong>Waiting for traction with no runway is a bet you can lose.</strong> Your cash is already near zero — if you wait to raise on a better valuation, you're insolvent before the line proves itself. Timing only works if you can survive the wait; here you can't.`,
                    coach_r4_late:          `<strong>Raising only when cash runs short is the worst timing.</strong> By then you're negotiating from weakness mid-crisis, on bad terms, while the ramp stalls and SLAs slip. Finance has to be timed ahead of the need, not scrambled after it.`,
                    coach_r4_earlyequity:   `<strong>You sold 25% to fund a ramp you hadn't yet proven.</strong> At pre-traction valuation that's the low point — six months on, the Northgate line humming, the business is worth far more and you've handed away a quarter cheap. Could traction, or a bridge, have bought a better deal?`,
                    coach_r4_undercover:    `<strong>Your structure leaves most of the gap open.</strong> Covering only a fraction of the £1.4M need means the cash crunch still arrives mid-ramp — you've timed and chosen the instrument but not actually closed the funding gap. Bridge the whole gap or the company still runs out of cash.`,

