                    insight_scaled:         `Scaled and resilient: you grew the venture meaningfully AND raised its survival odds — funded the cash gap, broke the founder bottleneck, and managed concentration. This is the textbook owner-managed transition from founder-led to managed, done well.`,
                    insight_disciplined:    `Disciplined growth: you grew at a rate your cash and your founder could survive. You may have left some revenue on the table, but the venture is materially more likely to still be here in two years — and that is the right trade at this inflection point.`,
                    insight_fragile:        `You grew but became fragile. Revenue is up, but survival odds are thin — most likely an aggressive path against a thin cash gap, concentration left unprotected, or the founder bottleneck never broken. Growth that imperils survival is not good growth.`,
                    insight_short:          `You stayed safe but sub-scale, or fell short on both. Pure caution is also a risk — a competitor takes Northgate and your momentum cools. Revisit whether you built a resilient path to growth rather than just avoiding the risk.`,
                    insight_insolvent:      `Insolvent mid-ramp: the cash runway went negative. You were profitable on paper and out of cash before the first big Northgate invoice was paid — exactly the funding-gap failure the case warns of. Growth eats cash; the gap had to be funded first.`,
                    insight_bottleneck:     `Founder capacity stayed low: too many decisions still route through Priya. At scale that throttles the ramp and risks burnout and SLA penalties — scaling revenue requires scaling the decision-making, not just winning the contract.`,
                    insight_concentration:  `Concentration risk is unmanaged: a single customer now dominates revenue with no protection. A loss or a price squeeze at renewal could be existential — resilience demands caps, minimum terms and diversification.`,
                    insight_equity:         `You diluted the founder to strengthen the venture. Equity cost Priya ownership but bought resilience and expertise the firm needed to survive the scale — a defensible trade when timed against real cash need and valuation.`,
                    insight_transition:     `You completed the transition: growth, survival odds and founder capacity all healthy together. Harrow & Vale has moved from a firm that depends on Priya doing everything to one that can scale because others can decide.`,

                    hint_insolvent:         `The cash runway is negative — you're insolvent mid-ramp despite being profitable. Fund the £1.4M gap before the ramp, or phase the growth to fit the cash you actually have.`,
